      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but i feel like that's a bit too \"textbook.\" I'm trying to get into the more realistic, granular concerns that people in these neighborhoods actually voice. Like, when you look at the actual sociological shifts, isn't there a noticeable tension when the \"cultural baseline\" changes? For example, people often talk about how things like local business types, noise levels, or even just the general \"feel\" of the streets change in a way that makes the original residents feel like they don't belong in their own community anymore. Wouldn't you say that's a form of social friction that inherently threatens the existing stability of the area?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
