      "content": "Right, that \"erosion of social fabric\" part is important. I'm curious though, from a research perspective—if we look at the data or historical patterns, is there a specific trend where these shifts actually lead to a decline in certain types of community stability or safety? I've heard arguments that when the core demographic identity of a neighborhood changes so rapidly, it can lead to a breakdown in informal social controls and a loss of the shared values that keep an area cohesive. Does the research suggest that high-density immigration from significantly different cultural backgrounds tends to increase this kind of instability compared to more gradual or homogeneous shifts?",
